WEBVTT

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Whitney Hodges: Hi, Rose.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.000
rose: Hello?

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Whitney Hodges: Hi, how are you? I'm Whitney.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
rose: Hey, good morning. Hi.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Whitney Hodges: Good morning. It's nice to meet you.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.000
rose: Nice to meet you too, Whitney.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, I'm glad that you were able to find a time that worked and get a call booked in.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.000
rose: Yeah, me too. Mornings are always the best. I have two little ones, and so…

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
rose: It can be chaos in the afternoon, so…

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Whitney Hodges: I totally understand. How old are your kiddos?

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.000
rose: Yeah.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000
rose: Um, so they are 9 weeks and 2 and a half.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Whitney Hodges: Oh, wow. Oh, my goodness. Yeah, you've got your hands full.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.000
rose: Yeah.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.000
rose: Yes.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:40.000
rose: Yes, I definitely do. It's why, um, I could teach myself Meta, or I could possibly outsource this. So, that's part of why I'm here, is, um, honestly, just capacity, and, you know, there's the time-money matrix, and…

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Whitney Hodges: Right. Got it. Okay. Well, that was definitely one of my questions for you. So I'm Whitney. I'm one of the co-founders and co-owners of Wedding Industry Ads. I do really run the day-to-day of the business and have a pretty good pulse on everything that's going on with our clients. I like to stay very involved in that regard.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.000
rose: This is one of them. Tom.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.000
rose: Yes.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.000
rose: Okay.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, but I do have a full team of amazing women that are, um, behind me as well. I couldn't do it myself. Um, I'm based in Texas. Um, prior to being a digital advertising agency owner, which I've been doing now for 6 years, I had a 12-year career.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000
rose: Yeah.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
rose: Okay.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Whitney Hodges: in Austin in the wedding and events industry, um, in sales and catering sales, event sales, um, things along those lines. So, our call today, really, it's just a casual conversation. It's a no-pressure call. It's a chance for us to get to know one another, get to know each other's businesses.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.000
rose: Okay.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.000
rose: Yeah.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.000
rose: Yeah.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Whitney Hodges: Kind of gauge and see, you know, if working together may be a good fit, and then kind of determine how to continue the conversation from there.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.000
rose: Okay.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
rose: Yeah, that sounds wonderful. Thanks, Whitney. Yeah.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Whitney Hodges: Awesome. Okay, so I would love to hear a little bit more from you, and you kind of already shared just a little bit about, you know, kind of what's piqued your interest with ads, why now?

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.000
rose: Yes.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, so feel free to share more.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:18.000
rose: Okay. Um, so I… I've also been in the industry for 6 years, or been a photographer for 6 years. I am… before this, I had a career in, like, foreign policy, I was working internationally, and…

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Whitney Hodges: Wow. Mm-hmm.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.000
rose: When COVID happened, obviously that…

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
rose: didn't work anymore. So, in 2020, I was like, alright, I'm gonna start this wedding photography business in… at that point in time.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.000
rose: I am in Colorado, so predominantly like elopements, adventure elopements was jam. And what I've…

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:47.000
rose: since sort of learned, I think, in part through, like, becoming a parent, and then in part just, like, the natural sort of progression of how things are moving.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.000
rose: Personally, is that I'm more interested in doing.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.000
rose: like.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:09.000
rose: fewer higher-end weddings that are, like, more organized with a team and less of, like, I have to organize everything with the elopement. So what's, what sort of is evolving, and this has been happening somewhat organically through, well, through.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:16.000
rose: through intention, um, in the last 18 months is a transition from elopements to weddings. Um…

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.000
rose: Where I am finding the like…

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:27.000
rose: difficulty in getting good leads is… is definitely because I have raised prices somewhat.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:34.000
rose: And then secondly is just a little bit of… I think I'm not connecting in the same way, like…

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.000
rose: I'm having a hard time finding those clients, and so…

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:46.000
rose: A little bit of that is, I think, networking. A little bit of that is just, like, what I'm showing up as online. So there could be a messaging, um…

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Whitney Hodges: No.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:54.000
rose: Issue there, but I also know that, um, you know, a well-run ad…

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:02.000
rose: can make a big difference for the visibility and the trust. So I have run Google Ads in the past.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:12.000
rose: for kind of the elopement space, and they have been somewhat successful. They're just kept getting more and more expensive, and I was like, this is just really eating into my…

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:21.000
rose: profit. Um, like, I was… I would spend, you know, $900 and get one good lead, and then it's like, oh man, that just…

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.000
rose: that's a lot, you know? Um…

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Whitney Hodges: No.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:31.000
rose: So, yes, so now I'm kind of in this space of, like, I would like to run…

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:45.000
rose: And the other part I should say is that I am not very, like, organized on the data side of, like, the ads, and so I would, like… I would see my click-through rates, I would see my impressions, and I would see…

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:59.000
rose: you know, I would know what my conversions are, but I would not necessarily be, like, beta testing this ad versus this ad, and, like, seeing where the differences in the success rates are, and then, like, learning from that. So…

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:54.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:12.000
rose: that is something that, you know, I know a marketing agency would do, and they would be able to say, okay, like, this is what seems to be really working, and this is not. This is where we need to put our efforts. So…

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.000
rose: Um…

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.000
rose: So yeah, I…

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.000
rose: don't have much experience with Meta. I, um, have, like, boosted a post or two before, but nothing that's…

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
rose: strategized. So, um…

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.000
Whitney Hodges: Yes.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:39.000
rose: I am also struggling to, I think, connect with more Gen Z, so I know that Meta is probably a useful platform more so than it was to me four years ago.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Whitney Hodges: Yes.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.000
Whitney Hodges: Yes.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:55.000
rose: So yeah, all that's to say. You know, I know that ads are — you can spend good money running them. I know that there is a good ROI if they're done correctly. I just don't fully know how to do that.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Whitney Hodges: Yes, okay, yeah, I hear you and totally understand. Yeah, figuring out the ads piece of it can turn into a full-time job in and of itself. And when you're trying to kind of, like, do it alone or, like, DIY it.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.000
rose: Yeah.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:30.000
Whitney Hodges: like, a couple of things can happen, right? You can really kind of, like, feel a bit in the dark, and not really kind of know, is this working the way it should be? Is it not working the way it should be? Or it can also just turn into something where things slip through the cracks, right? Like, you're a photographer, you have an actual business to run, not just, like.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.000
rose: Yep.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:52.000
Whitney Hodges: launching new ads, you know, every couple of weeks to be able to split test and try out new creatives and things like that. So, I hear you, for sure. Um, I'm glad that you mentioned the messaging piece of it. That is so super important, and it's an area where we really spend a good amount of focus with, uh, on our clients, really before.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.000
rose: So, yeah.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.000
rose: Okay.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:08:11.000
Whitney Hodges: we even launch ads, right? Because it's so important that, you know, the first touchpoint that people see with an ad resonates, but that there's a through line, you know, all the way through from the ad to the landing page that they're hitting, to what their experience is like once they.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.000
rose: That makes sense. Yeah.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:26.000
Whitney Hodges: you know, inquire with you, have a sales conversation with you, all of that, right? And so sometimes we can see that if one thing is off somewhere, right, that that's the leak in the funnel. That's what's causing things not to work.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.000
rose: Right.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.000
rose: Right.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:43.000
Whitney Hodges: And sometimes it can be on the front end with the ads, um, but more often than not, it can be beyond the ads, right? What's being communicated on the landing page, um, what's even, you know, in the first email that people are receiving, or the, you know, the pricing guide, or packages, or things like that, that they are.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.000
rose: Right.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.000
rose: Right.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:53.000
Whitney Hodges: are checking out. So, when we work with our clients through, through Elevate, it is a very high-touch and, um, holistic, um, approach.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.000
rose: Okay.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:08.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, so we are really focusing on the paid piece of it, right? We're not doing anything as far as, like, your social media or anything like that, but, um, we also understand how it all really kind of works together, and what's happening on the paid side is gonna.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.000
rose: Right.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:18.000
Whitney Hodges: influence, um, and can positively impact, you know, what you see as far as, like, organic social media engagement. Um, things along those lines.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:31.000
rose: Yeah, I mean, that all makes, I know that that is part of it, right? You can't just run an ad and expect magic. It has to be, it has to resonate and it has to balance. So that makes sense. That makes full sense.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:52.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah. Mm-hmm. Yeah. Awesome. Yeah, the other thing that we see as well, and it's a question that I get a lot from some potential clients, is like, should I be running Google Ads? Should I be running Meta Ads? Should I be running both? And more often than not, we do see that when you are running to the both platforms in conjunction with one another, it has like a compounding effect, right?

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.000
rose: Yeah.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.000
rose: Okay.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:10:09.000
Whitney Hodges: Because when you think about them, they're two completely different platforms. Google is a search engine. People are going there with high intent, looking for what they're looking for, versus Meta being social media. On Facebook and Instagram, people are going there to be.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:29.000
Whitney Hodges: entertain, to connect with friends and family. So it's more of that disruptor marketing on that platform, right? Where, like, the copy and the creative are so important to kind of grab their attention. But, like, psychologically, people who find you through a Google search versus people who find you through a Meta search, they can be in kind of a different.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.000
rose: Right.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.000
rose: Okay.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.000
rose: Yep.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:47.000
Whitney Hodges: state of mind, right, and can kind of behave differently. Um, but, you know, we'll see, too, that somebody may find you on, um, you know, an Instagram ad, and maybe they're not ready to click through right away, or maybe they click over to your profile instead to do more research, or they then Google you.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.000
rose: Okay.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.000
rose: Absolutely.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:11:08.000
Whitney Hodges: You know, look up your reviews, things like that, or vice versa. Somebody finds you through a Google ad or organic Google search. They're not ready to inquire just yet, but when we have retargeting set up with the Meta Pixel, then we can make it so that when they open up their Instagram or their Facebook, they're being served an ad from you so that you're really kind of like continuing to.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:48.000
rose: Yeah.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:07.000
rose: Right.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:19.000
Whitney Hodges: stay top of mind and stay in front of them. So it's a way to really kind of engineer your visibility, and we like to call it, like, a full funnel of ads that we're creating.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.000
rose: Yeah, that makes sense. Yeah. Okay. Okay.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah. Mm-hmm. Yeah.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:38.000
Whitney Hodges: Now, um, the other piece of it with, um, with Elevate, um, is that we do create for our clients their ads traffic landing pages. Um, so I'm not sure if you've experimented with this at all with the ads that you have run, like, have you just been sending that traffic to.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:47.000
Whitney Hodges: Like, the homepage of your website, or a specific page of your site, or have you created a page specifically for that ads traffic?

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:56.000
rose: Yeah, I have created specific landing pages for the ads. I realized that, like, part of it is that I kind of serve two markets in the sense of.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:04.000
rose: like, I have kind of a home base in Alaska, so the home page wasn't the right answer, so I created landing pages.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.000
Whitney Hodges: Got it.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:14.000
rose: Um, so there are… my Colorado Wedding Photographer landing page is specific for the ads that I would like to run, and I have kind of been tweaking it, and…

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:24.000
rose: adjusting it and updating it. So, can you walk me through what you mean by creating these ad-specific landing pages?

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:40.000
Whitney Hodges: Yes, yeah. So, what we have found from working with clients for years, right, in a variety of different capacities, where, you know, when some arrangements, we just launch their ads, and others, we are launching their ads and continuing to manage them.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:13:00.000
Whitney Hodges: But our clients who we're seeing that have the most success, not only are we running their ads, but we also set up their funnels for them in our lead management platform called LeadLock, which is our version of GoHighLevel. I'm not sure if you're familiar with GoHighLevel or have heard of that platform before.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.000
rose: Okay.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:12:59.000
rose: Yep.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:19.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, it's a very robust platform, well-trusted, kind of across a variety of different industries. What we love about it is, um, there are just so many cool things that you can do with it, but first and foremost, from an advertising perspective, is the attribution data that you can see there.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.000
rose: Okay.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.000
rose: Okay.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:35.000
Whitney Hodges: With the way that we can set up the forms and UTM parameters inside of your campaigns, we're able to see which specific leads are coming from your ads. And not just that it's come through an ad, right, or come through the ads traffic landing page, but that it's come through.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.000
rose: Okay.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:53.000
Whitney Hodges: a Google ad, or a meta ad, or this particular meta ad creative. So, it's really valuable being able to see, like, exactly where they came in from, and then, of course, you know, what ends up happening with them, right? Their responsiveness, if they actually end up.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.000
rose: Okay.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:51.000
rose: Yeah.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Whitney Hodges: booking a call with you, turning into a booked client. So, it's a platform that handles landing pages, so we would build an ads traffic landing page for you there, really kind of based on what we see that's kind of working well across the industry.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:32.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, we can definitely kind of take into account what you currently have with your existing page, or if you really, like, love your page that you have right now, we could stick with that and just embed a Leadlock inquiry form on the page. Um, but your, you know, leads would be captured through Leadlock, and then everything would flow through there.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.000
rose: Okay.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:24.000
rose: Okay.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:33.000
rose: Okay.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:51.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, so we set up inquiry, nurture, email, and text sequences for every lead that comes in. Um, your calendar can be connected to LeadLock as well. Um, I assume that kind of part of your sales process is that you invite people to schedule a call or a Zoom with you, is that right? Yeah.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.000
rose: Okay.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:52.000
rose: Yes. Yeah. Yeah.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm, yes.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:15:02.000
rose: And I have workflows like that set up through Dubsado, so it sounds like it's… does it integrate through Dubsado, or could I… is it a whole separate now, a whole separate platform to then…

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.000
Whitney Hodges: Mmhm.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:20.000
Whitney Hodges: It is a separate platform, yeah. And so, we use Dubsado as well. What we use Dubsado for is more strictly, like, the client management piece of it. So, it's like, we'll use LeadLock for all of our marketing, right? So, for example, you know, you inquired with us.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:03.000
rose: Max.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.000
rose: Okay.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:35.000
Whitney Hodges: the emails that you were receiving, texts that you were receiving, that's all coming through LeadLock, you know, your ability to be able to schedule a time on my calendar, all through LeadLock, right? Um, but if and when we decide to work together, like, the contract and invoice that I will send you.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.000
rose: Okay.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:41.000
Whitney Hodges: will be from Dubsado, right? And you'll fill out the onboarding questionnaire there, right? Mm-hmm.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:43.000
rose: Okay. So it's just a… it basically just, like, precedes any Dubsado. Okay.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:48.000
Whitney Hodges: Right, yeah, so we think of it as, like, a true lead management platform.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:45.000
rose: Right.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:49.000
rose: Okay. Okay.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:16:09.000
Whitney Hodges: And, you know, we recommend it for our clients because it's what's able to give our team the visibility into seeing what's going on beyond just the ads, right? Because if we were to just run your ads in isolation, we may be able to look at the ad account and say, oh, the click-through rate is amazing, and the cost per click is great, and we're seeing all of these.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.000
rose: Yeah.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:15.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, you know, inquiries happening, all of these conversions, but without being able to see.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:31.000
Whitney Hodges: who those leads actually are. Are they responding to your emails and text messages? Are they actually booking calls? Are they actually becoming booked clients? We're really only able to look at one piece of the puzzle. Um, and the ads piece, of course, is important.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.000
rose: Yeah.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:38.000
Whitney Hodges: But us being able to have that full visibility is what gives us the ability to really be able to drive great results.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:39.000
rose: Okay.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:40.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:51.000
rose: I mean, that makes sense. On one hand, I'm like, gosh, I mean, I… I'm not… and maybe this is a problem you'll fix for me, but I'm not being flooded with leads such that, like, it's a…

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:58.000
rose: Now, I know what you're saying is more on the data side, so I understand that, but, like, I'm not getting 30 leads a month. I'm getting…

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.000
Whitney Hodges: Right. Mm-hmm.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.000
Whitney Hodges: Right.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:01.000
rose: 5, maybe 10. So…

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:00.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:10.000
rose: But I recognize that understanding the source of the lead is all helpful in retargeting.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.000
Whitney Hodges: Uh-huh.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:14.000
rose: So I suppose I'd be interested in it, in the lead. I just sort of am like…

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:13.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:24.000
rose: capacity-wise, like, it's not like I can't… I can't send them a text versus an automated system, but maybe that's getting ahead of the game here a little.

